{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540795061,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2f7724b001Un3KS640LBUOS4","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","messageID":"msg_1d2f76267001UI4ZQ8M9q3rAsx","type":"tool","id":"call_c8b89463e4764a07954ee76f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md","limit":120},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md, lines 1-120\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that generate one table of tracked commands, attach a typed command to the top recommendation, put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row, measure the typed block in the command capture and add the mirror visibility guard, hook health, a head length test and a brief cost script.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"typed command delivery tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a live model session, an arena or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `BLD` is `npm run build` in `ADV`, which must exit 0.\n31: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` its test `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `VCA` is `npx vitest run tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n32: - `DB` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`. `DBC` is `node $DB --check | jq -r .status`, which must print `fresh`. `CBR` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/README.md`.\n33: - `FU` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`. `FT` is `$ADV/tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` (new) and `VFT` is `npx vitest run tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `AR` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`. `ART` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` (new) and `VART` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n35: - `HT` is `$ADV/tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` (new) and `VHT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n36: - `CAP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`. `BASE` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-routing-baseline.json`. `CORPUS` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl`. `RS` is `npx vitest run tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md`.\n37: - `MBC` is `$ADV/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` (new). `OF` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs`. `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`.\n38: - `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`. `ST` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-status.ts`. `STT` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts` and `VST` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n39: - `SRM` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs`, `SRMT` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs` and `SRR` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/README.md`.\n40: - `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts`. `DSY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup.yaml`. `DCT` is `node --test $DS/tests/doctor-runtime-mirrors-contract.test.cjs $DS/tests/doctor-setup-orphan-line.test.cjs`. `IG` is `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n41: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print at least `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n42: - `EVAL` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`, which must print numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5.\n43: - `PY` is `python3 $ADV/tests/python/test_skill_advisor.py`, which must exit 0. `PAR` is `npx vitest run tests/parity/python-ts-parity.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed.\n44: \n45: Rules for every task:\n46: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it.\n47: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A script filters on `split` before it uses a prompt and prints row ids only.\n48: - No worker starts a live model session. Running the Claude prompt hook or the advisor CLI is not a model session, and both run in a sandboxed socket directory here.\n49: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T024 checks it.\n50: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, edits `AGENTS.md`, renames a command or edits a file under `.skilled/commands/create/`.\n51: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n52: \n53: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n54: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`), T002 (`$SP/typed-before.json`). Read-only for the repository.\n55: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that phases 005, 008 and 010 are committed, because they edit `AR`, `CA`, `ST`, `SCH`, `DSY`, `IG` and `RA`.\n56: - Wave 1: T003 (`DB`, `CA`), T004 (`SRM`, `SRMT`), T005 (`SCH`), T006 (`MBC`), T007 (`IG`).\n57: - Wave 2: T008 (`CA`, `CAT`), T009 (`ST`, `STT`), T010 (`DSY`), T011 (`SRR`).\n58: - Wave 3: T012 (`FU`, `FT`), T013 (`AR`, `ART`), T014 (`HT`).\n59: - Wave 4: T015 (`CAP`), T016 (`AR`).\n60: - Wave 5: T017 (`BASE`), T018 (`RA`), T019 (`CBR`).\n61: - Wave 6, one at a time because both start a sandboxed daemon: T020 (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`), T021 (`$SP/brief-cost.json`).\n62: - Verification, one at a time: T022, T023, T024, T025.\n63: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n68: ## Phase 1: Setup\n69: \n70: - [x] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3, .metrics.typed_reach_top3, .metrics.agreement, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached, .attachedMetrics.plain'`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `DBC`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VST`, `node --test $SRMT`, `node $SRM --check` and `DCT`, and write each command with its last output line or exit code into `before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` and a fourth no higher than 5, `TC` and `PY` exit 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `node $SRM --check` exits 0 and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 15. When a gate is red here, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because a red before number cannot be held.\n71: - [x] T002 Record each typed row's state (`$SP/typed-before.json`): run `node $CAP | jq '[.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key | endswith(\"-typed-01\")) | {id: .key, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1}] as $t | .attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [$t[] | . + {attached: ($a[.id] // null)}]' > $SP/typed-before.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 44, `jq '[.[] | select(.top1)] | length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 24 and `jq -r '.[] | keys[]' $SP/typed-before.json | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' '` prints `attached id top top1 `.\n72: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n77: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n78: \n79: ### Wave 1\n80: - [ ] T003 [P] Generate one table of every tracked command (`DB`, `CA`). In `CA`, above `// BEGIN GENERATED COMMAND ROWS`, add `export interface TrackedCommand { readonly policy: 'run' | 'start' | 'ask'; readonly ownerSkillId: string; readonly scorerRow: boolean; }` with a comment saying the table covers every tracked command, whether or not its bridge is active, so a typed command always has a policy and an owner. In `DB`'s `renderCommandRows(entries, policies)`, build a third map over every entry, with no enabled filter: key `entryRunnable(entry)`, value `{ policy: runnablePolicy(runnable, policies), ownerSkillId: entry.skillId, scorerRow: entry.runtime.typescript.enabled === true }`. Throw `[command-bridges] Conflicting tracked command <runnable>` when one runnable repeats with a different owner or policy. Render it after the `COMMAND_POLICIES` block as `export const TRACKED_COMMANDS: Readonly<Record<string, TrackedCommand>> = {` with one line per key in sorted order, shaped `  '<runnable>': { policy: '<policy>', ownerSkillId: '<owner>', scorerRow: <true|false> },`, and `};`. Run `node $DB`. Do not edit the generated block by hand. Verify: `DBC` prints `fresh`, `rg -c \"scorerRow: (true|false) \\},$\" $CA` prints at least 44, `rg -n \"'/create:goal': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'sk-doc', scorerRow: false \\},\" $CA` and `rg -n \"'/speckit:resume': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit', scorerRow: true \\},\" $CA` each print one line, `TC` exits 0, `npx vitest run tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- $ADV/scripts/skill_advisor.py $ADV/lib/scorer/projection.ts` prints nothing.\n81: - [ ] T004 [P] Add a command visibility count and a collapse guard to the mirror check (`SRM`, `SRMT`). Add `const COLLAPSE_ALLOWLIST = new Set(['create/skill.md'])` with a comment saying Claude Code reads a command folder holding `skill.md` as one skill and hides its siblings, and the existing case is kept until the commands are renamed. Add and export `commandVisibility(expectedLinks)`: it takes the `claude-commands` links whose path below `.claude/commands/` holds a folder, counts them as `total`, and counts Claude's `visible` as one per folder that holds `skill.md` plus every command in the other folders. Cursor's `visible` is `total`, because Cursor uses flat names. Add and export `collapseDrift(expectedLinks)`, which returns `COLLAPSE <folder>/skill.md hides <n> sibling commands on Claude Code` for each folder holding `skill.md` whose path is not in the allowlist. In `checkMirrors`, add those lines to `drift`, and before the PASS line print `[runtime-mirror-sync] visibility claude <visible>/<total>, cursor <total>/<total>`. Keep the PASS line as it is. In `SRMT`, add one case where synthetic links for `create/skill.md`, `create/readme.md` and `deep/review.md` give `{ claude: { visible: 2, total: 3 }, cursor: { visible: 3, total: 3 } }` and no drift, and one where an added `design/skill.md` beside `design/chart.md` gives `['COLLAPSE design/skill.md hides 1 sibling commands on Claude Code']`. Verify: `node --test $SRMT` reports `fail 0`, `node $SRM --check` prints a line containing `visibility claude 32/44, cursor 44/44` and exits 0, and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n82: - [ ] T005 [P] Add `hookHealth` to the status output schema (`SCH`). In `AdvisorStatusOutputSchema`, after `embeddingsHealth`, add `hookHealth: z.object({ invocations: z.number().int().positive(), rollingP95Ms: z.number().nonnegative(), rollingFailOpenRate: z.number().min(0).max(1), rollingCacheHitRate: z.number().min(0).max(1) }).strict().optional()`. Verify: `TC` exits 0, `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $SCH` prints 1 and `npx vitest run tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed.\n83: - [ ] T006 [P] Create `MBC`. `node $MBC [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` reads `CORPUS` under `--root` (default the current directory), keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, and with `--rows` keeps only the named ids, exiting 2 with `{\"error\":\"holdout_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for a row whose split is `holdout` and `{\"error\":\"unknown_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for an id it cannot find, before it spawns anything. It makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-cost-'))`, and for each row pipes `{\"prompt\":<prompt>,\"cwd\":<root>,\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cost-<id>\"}` into `node <root>/$HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to that directory and a 30,000 ms timeout. It takes `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` (or the empty string), and records `bytes` as its UTF-8 length and `estimatedTokens` as `Math.ceil(bytes / 4)`, the renderer's own four characters per token. It prints one JSON object `{ rows: [{ id, bytes, estimatedTokens }], totalBytes, medianBytes, maxBytes }` and never prints a prompt. At the end it sends `SIGTERM` only to a process whose command line names that directory and removes the directory. Verify: `node --check $MBC` exits 0, `node $MBC --rows cmd-speckit-save-typed-01 | jq -c '.rows[0] | [.id, (.bytes > 0), (.estimatedTokens == ((.bytes + 3) / 4 | floor))]'` prints `[\"cmd-speckit-save-typed-01\",true,true]`, `node $MBC --rows \"$(jq -r 'select(.split==\"holdout\") | .id' $CORPUS | head -1)\"` exits 2 and prints `holdout_row`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n84: - [ ] T007 [P] In `IG`, under `### Check the Install`, add one paragraph after the paragraph about Codex hook approval: to see the advisor answer a prompt, run `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the utils folder\"}' --format json | jq -r '.data.recommendations[0].skillId'`, which prints one skill id such as `sk-doc`, and when it prints nothing or `null`, run `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild`. Re-read the section first, because phase 008 also edits section 2. Verify: `rg -c \"advisor_recommend --json\" $IG` prints 1, and the documented command prints one line that is neither empty nor `null`.\n85: \n86: ### Wave 2\n87: - [ ] T008 [P] Add two typed command helpers (`CA`, `CAT`). `typedCommandIn(prompt)` lowercases the prompt and returns the longest `TRACKED_COMMANDS` key found at a position where the character before it is absent, whitespace, a backtick, a quote or `(`, and the character after it is absent or not a word character, colon or hyphen. It returns undefined when none is found. `typedCommandForTop(topSkillId, prompt)` takes that key and returns `{ command, policy, ownerSkillId }` from its row when `topSkillId` is the row's `ownerSkillId` or `commandRowFor(topSkillId)?.command` equals the key, and undefined otherwise. In `CAT`, add cases: `/create:skill-parent deploy-hub with three modes` gives `/create:skill-parent`, `/create:sk` gives undefined, `see docs/create:goal.md` gives undefined, `typedCommandForTop('system-spec-kit', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives `{ command: '/speckit:resume', policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit' }`, `typedCommandForTop('command-speckit-resume', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives the same and `typedCommandForTop('sk-doc', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives undefined. Verify: `VCA` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n88: - [ ] T009 [P] Report hook health in the status (`ST`, `STT`). Run after phase 008, which edits `readSemanticLaneHealth` and `handleAdvisorStatus` in the same file. In `readAdvisorStatus`, in the `output` object of the success path (the one inside `try`, right after `laneWeights: DEFAULT_SCORER_WEIGHTS,`), add `hookHealth` from `readAdvisorHookHealthSection(workspaceRoot)` (`$ADV/lib/metrics.ts`) when its `lastInvocations` is not empty: `invocations` is the length of `lastInvocations` and the three rolling fields copy over. Leave the error path as it is. Add a comment saying the p95 counts cache-hit invocations only. In `STT`, add one case that writes two valid diagnostic records with `persistAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord` for a temporary workspace root and reads `hookHealth.invocations` 2, and one case for a root with no records that reads no `hookHealth` key. Verify: `VST` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n89: - [ ] T010 [P] Show hook health under the setup advisor leg (`DSY`). Run after phase 008, which adds an embedder leg after this one. In the single list item that starts `- \"Leg advisor:`, append before its closing quote: ` When data.hookHealth is present, show hook p95 <rollingP95Ms> ms over <invocations> prompts and fail-open <rollingFailOpenRate as a percent> under the leg. It never changes the leg's status.` Change nothing else. Verify: `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $DSY` prints 1 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n90: - [ ] T011 [P] In `SRR` section 5, add two sentences after the check command: the check also prints how many tracked commands each runtime shows, and it fails when a command folder other than `create/` holds a `skill.md`, because Claude Code would then hide that folder's other commands. Verify: `rg -n \"skill.md\" $SRR` prints at least one line in section 5.\n91: \n92: ### Wave 3\n93: - [ ] T012 [P] Put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row (`FU`, `FT`). Import `typedCommandIn` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from `../command-attach.js`. Export `rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower)`: when `typedCommandIn(promptLower)` names a row with `scorerRow` false and that row's owner is at an index above 0 with `passes_threshold` true, return the list with the owner moved to index 0 and every other entry in its order. Otherwise return `ranked` unchanged. In `scoreAdvisorPrompt`, call `ranked = rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower);` right after the `applyDoctorHelperHandoff` statement and before `const passing`. Add a comment saying a typed command with no row of its own can only reach the reader through its owner, so the owner must lead. In `FT`, add cases on synthetic lists: `/create:goal x` with `sk-doc` passing at index 1 moves it first, the same with `sk-doc` not passing changes nothing, `/speckit:save x` (a command with a scorer row) changes nothing and a prompt with no slash changes nothing. Verify: `VFT` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n94: - [ ] T013 [P] Attach a typed command to the top recommendation (`AR`, `ART`). Import `typedCommandForTop` from `../lib/command-attach.js` and export `attachTypedCommand(output, prompt)`: when `output.recommendations[0]` exists and `typedCommandForTop(top.skillId, prompt)` returns a row, return `AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse` of the output with the first recommendation's `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId` set from that row, replacing any compiled route command. Otherwise return `output` itself. In `computeRecommendationOutput`, wrap both final returns, the cached one and the last one, as `return attachTypedCommand(<the existing expression>, input.prompt);`. Add a comment saying a typed command is the reader's own choice, so it outranks a routed mode. In `ART`, add cases: a top `system-spec-kit` with prompt `/speckit:resume cache work` carries `/speckit:resume`, `start` and `system-spec-kit`, a top `sk-doc` already carrying `/create:readme` with prompt `/create:goal retrofit` carries `/create:goal` and `start`, a top `mcp-tooling` with that prompt returns the same object and a prompt with no slash returns the same object. Verify: `VART` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n95: - [ ] T014 [P] Create `HT`. For every key of `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, render `renderAdvisorBrief` with one passing `skill` recommendation for the row's owner that carries the key as `command`, its `policy` and its `ownerSkillId`, freshness `live` and `metrics: { tokenCap: 80 }`, in the shape `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` uses. Do the same for every id in `COMMAND_ROWS` as a `command` recommendation. Take the head before `DIRECTIVES_LABEL` and assert that `Buffer.byteLength(head, 'utf8')` is at most `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT`, that the head does not end with `...` and that it contains `<command> (<policy>)`. Define `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT` as `80 * 4` with a comment naming the renderer's default token cap and its four characters per token. Add one case where a synthetic 330-character command fails the same check. Verify: `VHT` reports 0 failed and at least 45 passed tests.\n96: \n97: ### Wave 4\n98: - [ ] T015 Add the typed block to the command capture (`CAP`). Import `typedCommandForTop` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from the built attach module the script already loads. For each row with `row_type` `typed`, compute `delivered` as `typedCommandForTop(topId, row.prompt)?.command ?? attached`, the same precedence as the handler, and `policy` as `TRACKED_COMMANDS[delivered]?.policy ?? null`. Count `own` when `top1Hit`, else `attached` when `delivered` equals the expected command and `policy` is not null, in a tally built with `emptyAttachedTally` and `addAttachedRow`. Add `typed: attachedTallyJson(...)` to `attachedMetrics`, and `typedRows`, keyed by row id in sorted order, each `{ expected_command, top, carried, command, policy }`, where `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null. Write no prompt text. Leave every existing key and its computation unchanged. Verify: after `BLD`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed'` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `node $CAP | jq -c '[.attachedMetrics.typedRows[] | keys] | unique'` prints `[[\"carried\",\"command\",\"expected_command\",\"policy\",\"top\"]]` and `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n99: - [ ] T016 Take a hub-attached command's policy from the new table (`AR`). In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace the spread that reads `COMMAND_POLICIES` with `...(Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command) ? { policy: TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy } : {})`, import `TRACKED_COMMANDS` and drop `COMMAND_POLICIES` from the import when nothing else uses it. Verify: `rg -c \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" $AR` prints nothing or 0, `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS\\[command\\]\" $AR` prints 1, `TC` exits 0 and `VART` reports 0 failed.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 5\n102: - [ ] T017 Rebuild and recapture the command baseline (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}`, `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `jq '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3' $BASE` prints at least the value in `$SP/before.txt`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain' $BASE` equals the line in `$SP/before.txt` and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n103: - [ ] T018 [P] In `RA`, describe `attachedMetrics.typed` and `attachedMetrics.typedRows`, the rule that a typed command travels on its owner, and `measure-brief-cost.mjs` with its flags, its output keys and its sandbox. Verify: `rg -c \"attachedMetrics.typed|typedRows|measure-brief-cost\" $RA` prints at least 3.\n104: - [ ] T019 [P] In `CBR`, describe `TRACKED_COMMANDS`: one row per bridge entry with its policy, its owner and `scorerRow`, generated whether or not the bridge is active. Verify: `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS|scorerRow\" $CBR` prints at least 2.\n105: \n106: ### Wave 6\n107: - [ ] T020 Check the real heads (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`). Write a script that reads `CORPUS`, keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-typed-'))`, pipes each row's payload (the shape of T006, session id `typed-<id>`) into `node $HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to it, takes the first line of `additionalContext` and reads it with `parseOffer` from `OF`. It writes `[{ id, offeredCommand, offeredPolicy, commandMatches, policyPresent, labelAgrees }]` to `typed-heads.json`, where `commandMatches` compares the `/<namespace>:<name>` part of the offer with the row's `expected_command`, `policyPresent` is true for `run`, `start` or `ask` and `labelAgrees` compares the policy with the row's `expected_policy`. It stops and removes its sandbox as T006 does and prints no prompt. Verify: `jq '[.[] | select(.commandMatches and .policyPresent)] | length' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints 44, `jq -r '[.[] | select(.labelAgrees | not) | .id] | join(\",\")' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints `cmd-design-extract-typed-01,cmd-doctor-env-typed-01,cmd-doctor-update-typed-01` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-typed-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n108: - [ ] T021 Measure the brief cost (`$SP/brief-cost.json`). Run `node $MBC > $SP/brief-cost.json`. Verify: `jq '.rows | length' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints 44, `jq '.maxBytes > 0 and .medianBytes > 0' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints `true` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n109: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n114: ## Phase 3: Verification\n115: \n116: - [ ] T022 Run every floor. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt` and no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5, `TC` exits 0, `PY` exits 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VFT`, `VART`, `VHT` and `VST` report 0 failed, `node --test $SRMT` and `DCT` report `fail 0` and `node $SRM --check` exits 0. Write the outputs beside the before lines in `$SP/after.txt`.\n117: - [ ] T023 Read hook health live. Make one directory with `D=$(mktemp -d \"$TMPDIR/ssa-status-XXXXXX\")`, run `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR=\"$D\" node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --no-warm-only | jq -r '.data.hookHealth.rollingP95Ms | type'`, then stop only a process whose command line names `$D` and remove `$D`. The Claude prompt hook runs in T020 and T021 wrote the diagnostic records it reads. Verify: the command prints `number`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-status-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n118: - [ ] T024 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled INSTALL-SKILLED.md AGENTS.md` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n119: - [ ] T025 Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n120: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 121]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md, lines 1-120\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that generate one table of tracked commands, attach a typed command to the top recommendation, put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row, measure the typed block in the command capture and add the mirror visibility guard, hook health, a head length test and a brief cost script.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"typed command delivery tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a live model session, an arena or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `BLD` is `npm run build` in `ADV`, which must exit 0.\n31: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` its test `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `VCA` is `npx vitest run tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n32: - `DB` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`. `DBC` is `node $DB --check | jq -r .status`, which must print `fresh`. `CBR` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/README.md`.\n33: - `FU` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`. `FT` is `$ADV/tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` (new) and `VFT` is `npx vitest run tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `AR` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`. `ART` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` (new) and `VART` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n35: - `HT` is `$ADV/tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` (new) and `VHT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n36: - `CAP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`. `BASE` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-routing-baseline.json`. `CORPUS` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl`. `RS` is `npx vitest run tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md`.\n37: - `MBC` is `$ADV/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` (new). `OF` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs`. `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`.\n38: - `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`. `ST` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-status.ts`. `STT` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts` and `VST` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n39: - `SRM` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs`, `SRMT` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs` and `SRR` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/README.md`.\n40: - `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts`. `DSY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup.yaml`. `DCT` is `node --test $DS/tests/doctor-runtime-mirrors-contract.test.cjs $DS/tests/doctor-setup-orphan-line.test.cjs`. `IG` is `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n41: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print at least `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n42: - `EVAL` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`, which must print numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5.\n43: - `PY` is `python3 $ADV/tests/python/test_skill_advisor.py`, which must exit 0. `PAR` is `npx vitest run tests/parity/python-ts-parity.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed.\n44: \n45: Rules for every task:\n46: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it.\n47: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A script filters on `split` before it uses a prompt and prints row ids only.\n48: - No worker starts a live model session. Running the Claude prompt hook or the advisor CLI is not a model session, and both run in a sandboxed socket directory here.\n49: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T024 checks it.\n50: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, edits `AGENTS.md`, renames a command or edits a file under `.skilled/commands/create/`.\n51: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n52: \n53: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n54: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`), T002 (`$SP/typed-before.json`). Read-only for the repository.\n55: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that phases 005, 008 and 010 are committed, because they edit `AR`, `CA`, `ST`, `SCH`, `DSY`, `IG` and `RA`.\n56: - Wave 1: T003 (`DB`, `CA`), T004 (`SRM`, `SRMT`), T005 (`SCH`), T006 (`MBC`), T007 (`IG`).\n57: - Wave 2: T008 (`CA`, `CAT`), T009 (`ST`, `STT`), T010 (`DSY`), T011 (`SRR`).\n58: - Wave 3: T012 (`FU`, `FT`), T013 (`AR`, `ART`), T014 (`HT`).\n59: - Wave 4: T015 (`CAP`), T016 (`AR`).\n60: - Wave 5: T017 (`BASE`), T018 (`RA`), T019 (`CBR`).\n61: - Wave 6, one at a time because both start a sandboxed daemon: T020 (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`), T021 (`$SP/brief-cost.json`).\n62: - Verification, one at a time: T022, T023, T024, T025.\n63: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n68: ## Phase 1: Setup\n69: \n70: - [x] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3, .metrics.typed_reach_top3, .metrics.agreement, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached, .attachedMetrics.plain'`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `DBC`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VST`, `node --test $SRMT`, `node $SRM --check` and `DCT`, and write each command with its last output line or exit code into `before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` and a fourth no higher than 5, `TC` and `PY` exit 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `node $SRM --check` exits 0 and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 15. When a gate is red here, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because a red before number cannot be held.\n71: - [x] T002 Record each typed row's state (`$SP/typed-before.json`): run `node $CAP | jq '[.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key | endswith(\"-typed-01\")) | {id: .key, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1}] as $t | .attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [$t[] | . + {attached: ($a[.id] // null)}]' > $SP/typed-before.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 44, `jq '[.[] | select(.top1)] | length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 24 and `jq -r '.[] | keys[]' $SP/typed-before.json | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' '` prints `attached id top top1 `.\n72: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n77: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n78: \n79: ### Wave 1\n80: - [ ] T003 [P] Generate one table of every tracked command (`DB`, `CA`). In `CA`, above `// BEGIN GENERATED COMMAND ROWS`, add `export interface TrackedCommand { readonly policy: 'run' | 'start' | 'ask'; readonly ownerSkillId: string; readonly scorerRow: boolean; }` with a comment saying the table covers every tracked command, whether or not its bridge is active, so a typed command always has a policy and an owner. In `DB`'s `renderCommandRows(entries, policies)`, build a third map over every entry, with no enabled filter: key `entryRunnable(entry)`, value `{ policy: runnablePolicy(runnable, policies), ownerSkillId: entry.skillId, scorerRow: entry.runtime.typescript.enabled === true }`. Throw `[command-bridges] Conflicting tracked command <runnable>` when one runnable repeats with a different owner or policy. Render it after the `COMMAND_POLICIES` block as `export const TRACKED_COMMANDS: Readonly<Record<string, TrackedCommand>> = {` with one line per key in sorted order, shaped `  '<runnable>': { policy: '<policy>', ownerSkillId: '<owner>', scorerRow: <true|false> },`, and `};`. Run `node $DB`. Do not edit the generated block by hand. Verify: `DBC` prints `fresh`, `rg -c \"scorerRow: (true|false) \\},$\" $CA` prints at least 44, `rg -n \"'/create:goal': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'sk-doc', scorerRow: false \\},\" $CA` and `rg -n \"'/speckit:resume': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit', scorerRow: true \\},\" $CA` each print one line, `TC` exits 0, `npx vitest run tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- $ADV/scripts/skill_advisor.py $ADV/lib/scorer/projection.ts` prints nothing.\n81: - [ ] T004 [P] Add a command visibility count and a collapse guard to the mirror check (`SRM`, `SRMT`). Add `const COLLAPSE_ALLOWLIST = new Set(['create/skill.md'])` with a comment saying Claude Code reads a command folder holding `skill.md` as one skill and hides its siblings, and the existing case is kept until the commands are renamed. Add and export `commandVisibility(expectedLinks)`: it takes the `claude-commands` links whose path below `.claude/commands/` holds a folder, counts them as `total`, and counts Claude's `visible` as one per folder that holds `skill.md` plus every command in the other folders. Cursor's `visible` is `total`, because Cursor uses flat names. Add and export `collapseDrift(expectedLinks)`, which returns `COLLAPSE <folder>/skill.md hides <n> sibling commands on Claude Code` for each folder holding `skill.md` whose path is not in the allowlist. In `checkMirrors`, add those lines to `drift`, and before the PASS line print `[runtime-mirror-sync] visibility claude <visible>/<total>, cursor <total>/<total>`. Keep the PASS line as it is. In `SRMT`, add one case where synthetic links for `create/skill.md`, `create/readme.md` and `deep/review.md` give `{ claude: { visible: 2, total: 3 }, cursor: { visible: 3, total: 3 } }` and no drift, and one where an added `design/skill.md` beside `design/chart.md` gives `['COLLAPSE design/skill.md hides 1 sibling commands on Claude Code']`. Verify: `node --test $SRMT` reports `fail 0`, `node $SRM --check` prints a line containing `visibility claude 32/44, cursor 44/44` and exits 0, and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n82: - [ ] T005 [P] Add `hookHealth` to the status output schema (`SCH`). In `AdvisorStatusOutputSchema`, after `embeddingsHealth`, add `hookHealth: z.object({ invocations: z.number().int().positive(), rollingP95Ms: z.number().nonnegative(), rollingFailOpenRate: z.number().min(0).max(1), rollingCacheHitRate: z.number().min(0).max(1) }).strict().optional()`. Verify: `TC` exits 0, `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $SCH` prints 1 and `npx vitest run tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed.\n83: - [ ] T006 [P] Create `MBC`. `node $MBC [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` reads `CORPUS` under `--root` (default the current directory), keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, and with `--rows` keeps only the named ids, exiting 2 with `{\"error\":\"holdout_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for a row whose split is `holdout` and `{\"error\":\"unknown_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for an id it cannot find, before it spawns anything. It makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-cost-'))`, and for each row pipes `{\"prompt\":<prompt>,\"cwd\":<root>,\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cost-<id>\"}` into `node <root>/$HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to that directory and a 30,000 ms timeout. It takes `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` (or the empty string), and records `bytes` as its UTF-8 length and `estimatedTokens` as `Math.ceil(bytes / 4)`, the renderer's own four characters per token. It prints one JSON object `{ rows: [{ id, bytes, estimatedTokens }], totalBytes, medianBytes, maxBytes }` and never prints a prompt. At the end it sends `SIGTERM` only to a process whose command line names that directory and removes the directory. Verify: `node --check $MBC` exits 0, `node $MBC --rows cmd-speckit-save-typed-01 | jq -c '.rows[0] | [.id, (.bytes > 0), (.estimatedTokens == ((.bytes + 3) / 4 | floor))]'` prints `[\"cmd-speckit-save-typed-01\",true,true]`, `node $MBC --rows \"$(jq -r 'select(.split==\"holdout\") | .id' $CORPUS | head -1)\"` exits 2 and prints `holdout_row`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n84: - [ ] T007 [P] In `IG`, under `### Check the Install`, add one paragraph after the paragraph about Codex hook approval: to see the advisor answer a prompt, run `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the utils folder\"}' --format json | jq -r '.data.recommendations[0].skillId'`, which prints one skill id such as `sk-doc`, and when it prints nothing or `null`, run `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild`. Re-read the section first, because phase 008 also edits section 2. Verify: `rg -c \"advisor_recommend --json\" $IG` prints 1, and the documented command prints one line that is neither empty nor `null`.\n85: \n86: ### Wave 2\n87: - [ ] T008 [P] Add two typed command helpers (`CA`, `CAT`). `typedCommandIn(prompt)` lowercases the prompt and returns the longest `TRACKED_COMMANDS` key found at a position where the character before it is absent, whitespace, a backtick, a quote or `(`, and the character after it is absent or not a word character, colon or hyphen. It returns undefined when none is found. `typedCommandForTop(topSkillId, prompt)` takes that key and returns `{ command, policy, ownerSkillId }` from its row when `topSkillId` is the row's `ownerSkillId` or `commandRowFor(topSkillId)?.command` equals the key, and undefined otherwise. In `CAT`, add cases: `/create:skill-parent deploy-hub with three modes` gives `/create:skill-parent`, `/create:sk` gives undefined, `see docs/create:goal.md` gives undefined, `typedCommandForTop('system-spec-kit', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives `{ command: '/speckit:resume', policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit' }`, `typedCommandForTop('command-speckit-resume', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives the same and `typedCommandForTop('sk-doc', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives undefined. Verify: `VCA` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n88: - [ ] T009 [P] Report hook health in the status (`ST`, `STT`). Run after phase 008, which edits `readSemanticLaneHealth` and `handleAdvisorStatus` in the same file. In `readAdvisorStatus`, in the `output` object of the success path (the one inside `try`, right after `laneWeights: DEFAULT_SCORER_WEIGHTS,`), add `hookHealth` from `readAdvisorHookHealthSection(workspaceRoot)` (`$ADV/lib/metrics.ts`) when its `lastInvocations` is not empty: `invocations` is the length of `lastInvocations` and the three rolling fields copy over. Leave the error path as it is. Add a comment saying the p95 counts cache-hit invocations only. In `STT`, add one case that writes two valid diagnostic records with `persistAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord` for a temporary workspace root and reads `hookHealth.invocations` 2, and one case for a root with no records that reads no `hookHealth` key. Verify: `VST` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n89: - [ ] T010 [P] Show hook health under the setup advisor leg (`DSY`). Run after phase 008, which adds an embedder leg after this one. In the single list item that starts `- \"Leg advisor:`, append before its closing quote: ` When data.hookHealth is present, show hook p95 <rollingP95Ms> ms over <invocations> prompts and fail-open <rollingFailOpenRate as a percent> under the leg. It never changes the leg's status.` Change nothing else. Verify: `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $DSY` prints 1 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n90: - [ ] T011 [P] In `SRR` section 5, add two sentences after the check command: the check also prints how many tracked commands each runtime shows, and it fails when a command folder other than `create/` holds a `skill.md`, because Claude Code would then hide that folder's other commands. Verify: `rg -n \"skill.md\" $SRR` prints at least one line in section 5.\n91: \n92: ### Wave 3\n93: - [ ] T012 [P] Put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row (`FU`, `FT`). Import `typedCommandIn` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from `../command-attach.js`. Export `rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower)`: when `typedCommandIn(promptLower)` names a row with `scorerRow` false and that row's owner is at an index above 0 with `passes_threshold` true, return the list with the owner moved to index 0 and every other entry in its order. Otherwise return `ranked` unchanged. In `scoreAdvisorPrompt`, call `ranked = rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower);` right after the `applyDoctorHelperHandoff` statement and before `const passing`. Add a comment saying a typed command with no row of its own can only reach the reader through its owner, so the owner must lead. In `FT`, add cases on synthetic lists: `/create:goal x` with `sk-doc` passing at index 1 moves it first, the same with `sk-doc` not passing changes nothing, `/speckit:save x` (a command with a scorer row) changes nothing and a prompt with no slash changes nothing. Verify: `VFT` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n94: - [ ] T013 [P] Attach a typed command to the top recommendation (`AR`, `ART`). Import `typedCommandForTop` from `../lib/command-attach.js` and export `attachTypedCommand(output, prompt)`: when `output.recommendations[0]` exists and `typedCommandForTop(top.skillId, prompt)` returns a row, return `AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse` of the output with the first recommendation's `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId` set from that row, replacing any compiled route command. Otherwise return `output` itself. In `computeRecommendationOutput`, wrap both final returns, the cached one and the last one, as `return attachTypedCommand(<the existing expression>, input.prompt);`. Add a comment saying a typed command is the reader's own choice, so it outranks a routed mode. In `ART`, add cases: a top `system-spec-kit` with prompt `/speckit:resume cache work` carries `/speckit:resume`, `start` and `system-spec-kit`, a top `sk-doc` already carrying `/create:readme` with prompt `/create:goal retrofit` carries `/create:goal` and `start`, a top `mcp-tooling` with that prompt returns the same object and a prompt with no slash returns the same object. Verify: `VART` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n95: - [ ] T014 [P] Create `HT`. For every key of `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, render `renderAdvisorBrief` with one passing `skill` recommendation for the row's owner that carries the key as `command`, its `policy` and its `ownerSkillId`, freshness `live` and `metrics: { tokenCap: 80 }`, in the shape `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` uses. Do the same for every id in `COMMAND_ROWS` as a `command` recommendation. Take the head before `DIRECTIVES_LABEL` and assert that `Buffer.byteLength(head, 'utf8')` is at most `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT`, that the head does not end with `...` and that it contains `<command> (<policy>)`. Define `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT` as `80 * 4` with a comment naming the renderer's default token cap and its four characters per token. Add one case where a synthetic 330-character command fails the same check. Verify: `VHT` reports 0 failed and at least 45 passed tests.\n96: \n97: ### Wave 4\n98: - [ ] T015 Add the typed block to the command capture (`CAP`). Import `typedCommandForTop` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from the built attach module the script already loads. For each row with `row_type` `typed`, compute `delivered` as `typedCommandForTop(topId, row.prompt)?.command ?? attached`, the same precedence as the handler, and `policy` as `TRACKED_COMMANDS[delivered]?.policy ?? null`. Count `own` when `top1Hit`, else `attached` when `delivered` equals the expected command and `policy` is not null, in a tally built with `emptyAttachedTally` and `addAttachedRow`. Add `typed: attachedTallyJson(...)` to `attachedMetrics`, and `typedRows`, keyed by row id in sorted order, each `{ expected_command, top, carried, command, policy }`, where `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null. Write no prompt text. Leave every existing key and its computation unchanged. Verify: after `BLD`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed'` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `node $CAP | jq -c '[.attachedMetrics.typedRows[] | keys] | unique'` prints `[[\"carried\",\"command\",\"expected_command\",\"policy\",\"top\"]]` and `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n99: - [ ] T016 Take a hub-attached command's policy from the new table (`AR`). In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace the spread that reads `COMMAND_POLICIES` with `...(Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command) ? { policy: TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy } : {})`, import `TRACKED_COMMANDS` and drop `COMMAND_POLICIES` from the import when nothing else uses it. Verify: `rg -c \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" $AR` prints nothing or 0, `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS\\[command\\]\" $AR` prints 1, `TC` exits 0 and `VART` reports 0 failed.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 5\n102: - [ ] T017 Rebuild and recapture the command baseline (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}`, `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `jq '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3' $BASE` prints at least the value in `$SP/before.txt`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain' $BASE` equals the line in `$SP/before.txt` and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n103: - [ ] T018 [P] In `RA`, describe `attachedMetrics.typed` and `attachedMetrics.typedRows`, the rule that a typed command travels on its owner, and `measure-brief-cost.mjs` with its flags, its output keys and its sandbox. Verify: `rg -c \"attachedMetrics.typed|typedRows|measure-brief-cost\" $RA` prints at least 3.\n104: - [ ] T019 [P] In `CBR`, describe `TRACKED_COMMANDS`: one row per bridge entry with its policy, its owner and `scorerRow`, generated whether or not the bridge is active. Verify: `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS|scorerRow\" $CBR` prints at least 2.\n105: \n106: ### Wave 6\n107: - [ ] T020 Check the real heads (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`). Write a script that reads `CORPUS`, keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-typed-'))`, pipes each row's payload (the shape of T006, session id `typed-<id>`) into `node $HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to it, takes the first line of `additionalContext` and reads it with `parseOffer` from `OF`. It writes `[{ id, offeredCommand, offeredPolicy, commandMatches, policyPresent, labelAgrees }]` to `typed-heads.json`, where `commandMatches` compares the `/<namespace>:<name>` part of the offer with the row's `expected_command`, `policyPresent` is true for `run`, `start` or `ask` and `labelAgrees` compares the policy with the row's `expected_policy`. It stops and removes its sandbox as T006 does and prints no prompt. Verify: `jq '[.[] | select(.commandMatches and .policyPresent)] | length' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints 44, `jq -r '[.[] | select(.labelAgrees | not) | .id] | join(\",\")' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints `cmd-design-extract-typed-01,cmd-doctor-env-typed-01,cmd-doctor-update-typed-01` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-typed-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n108: - [ ] T021 Measure the brief cost (`$SP/brief-cost.json`). Run `node $MBC > $SP/brief-cost.json`. Verify: `jq '.rows | length' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints 44, `jq '.maxBytes > 0 and .medianBytes > 0' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints `true` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n109: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n114: ## Phase 3: Verification\n115: \n116: - [ ] T022 Run every floor. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt` and no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5, `TC` exits 0, `PY` exits 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VFT`, `VART`, `VHT` and `VST` report 0 failed, `node --test $SRMT` and `DCT` report `fail 0` and `node $SRM --check` exits 0. Write the outputs beside the before lines in `$SP/after.txt`.\n117: - [ ] T023 Read hook health live. Make one directory with `D=$(mktemp -d \"$TMPDIR/ssa-status-XXXXXX\")`, run `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR=\"$D\" node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --no-warm-only | jq -r '.data.hookHealth.rollingP95Ms | type'`, then stop only a process whose command line names `$D` and remove `$D`. The Claude prompt hook runs in T020 and T021 wrote the diagnostic records it reads. Verify: the command prints `number`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-status-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n118: - [ ] T024 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled INSTALL-SKILLED.md AGENTS.md` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n119: - [ ] T025 Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n120: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 121]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md\",\"limit\":120}"},"time":{"start":1794540794443,"end":1794540795061}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794540797015,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","part":{"id":"prt_1d2f77c56001GY8M9N75Cy7NB1","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","messageID":"msg_1d2f76267001UI4ZQ8M9q3rAsx","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"11b8b12b41b1fc1de1651fe1c7975e40ca2d5a28","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":17175,"output":121,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":384,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794540802045,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","part":{"id":"prt_1d2f78ffd001jSw1TPCPqDuLB2","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","messageID":"msg_1d2f77c5b0017Kshjun9lzWqEF","type":"step-start","snapshot":"f4eb45378a05c1f28cdca35595a96f2c0aa1b7e7"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540803842,"sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2f7954b001kyiY3ELMTWqRFg","sessionID":"ses_e2d08c8a5ffeWsmgsmbXYbtBtw","messageID":"msg_1d2f77c5b0017Kshjun9lzWqEF","type":"tool","id":"call_0f1bda9732b84ce18209e408","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md, lines 1-163\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy: Code README\"\n3: description: \"Code-facing README for .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\"\n6:   - \"code README\"\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n10: \n11: Operator and maintenance scripts for this skill.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: ### Purpose\n18: \n19: This README documents the code-bearing folder `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy` so operators can understand its role without opening every source file first. It follows the sk-doc skill README structure while staying focused on code navigation.\n20: \n21: ### Usage\n22: \n23: Use this file to identify the folder boundary, the likely verification path and the local source files that need sk-code conventions. Keep behavior details in source comments and higher-level workflow details in the owning `SKILL.md`.\n24: \n25: ### Key Statistics\n26: \n27: | Metric | Value |\n28: |---|---:|\n29: | Code files | 9 |\n30: | README scope | Direct files in this folder |\n31: | Audit context | Internal validation notes |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 2. QUICK START\n36: \n37: **Step 1: Confirm the owner.**\n38: \n39: Start with `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md` for runtime routing and workflow boundaries.\n40: \n41: **Step 2: Inspect the local code.**\n42: \n43: ```bash\n44: rg --files .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Expected result: the command lists the source files summarized below.\n48: \n49: **Step 3: Verify changes.**\n50: \n51: Run individual scripts from the repository root with the documented arguments.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. FEATURES\n56: \n57: | Feature | What It Does |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Folder boundary | Documents direct code files under `runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy`. |\n60: | sk-code alignment | Points reviewers at OpenCode naming, header, error-handling and type-discipline checks. |\n61: | Verification handoff | Records the expected owner and audit packet for follow-up work. |\n62: \n63: ### Attribution on every run line\n64: \n65: Each line a behaviour run appends carries three fields beside the locator, exit and changed paths:\n66: \n67: - `offeredCommand` is the slash command the advisor head named, or `null` when the head named none.\n68: - `offeredPolicy` is the policy that directly follows that command, one of `run`, `start` or `ask`, or `null` when no policy follows it.\n69: - `briefDelivered` says whether the advisor brief reached the session. It is `true` or `false` where the runtime keeps a readable record, and `unknown` where none exists or cannot be read, so a gap in the record never reads as a miss.\n70: \n71: The offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn instead of a copy of the live session head. The head is the only text that names both the command and the policy, and not every runtime keeps it, so `replayOffer` runs the checkout's own hook and records what that checkout would have offered at the time of the run. A replay that finds no hook, a failing hook or a head without an offer writes null fields plus an `offerError` of `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`, so one bad line never aborts a run.\n72: \n73: ### Backfill a finished log\n74: \n75: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` adds those three fields to a log written before they existed, and a `--backfill` run without `--out` is a usage error that exits 2. The mode refuses with exit 2 and a JSON body holding its `code`:\n76: \n77: - `same_path` when `--out` resolves to the input log, because the backfill writes a new file.\n78: - `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when the corpus refuses a row id, because every prompt is resolved before the first line is written and a refused row leaves no output file behind.\n79: \n80: A line whose locator names no checkout keeps null offer fields and `offerError` `no_checkout`, and the rest of the log is still written with `offerBackfilled: true`.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n85: \n86: | Path | Purpose |\n87: |---|---|\n88: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n89: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n90: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n91: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. |\n92: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. `--row-id <id>` narrows either mode to one row and `--json` prints machine-readable output. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerUnknown`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n93: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input. |\n94: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy` and `briefDelivered` instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), or a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`). |\n95: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n100: \n101: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n102: |---|---|---|\n103: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n104: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n105: \n106: ---\n107: \n108: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n109: \n110: **Audit this folder**\n111: \n112: ```text\n113: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n114: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n115: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n116: ```\n117: \n118: **Score captured runs**\n119: \n120: ```bash\n121: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n122:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n123: ```\n124: \n125: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n126: \n127: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n128: \n129: ```bash\n130: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n131: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n132:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n133: ```\n134: \n135: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n136: \n137: **Backfill a finished log**\n138: \n139: ```bash\n140: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n141:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n142: ```\n143: \n144: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n145: \n146: ---\n147: \n148: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n149: \n150: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n151: |---|---|---|\n152: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n153: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n158: \n159: | Document | Purpose |\n160: |---|---|\n161: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n162: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n163: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md, lines 1-163\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy: Code README\"\n3: description: \"Code-facing README for .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\"\n6:   - \"code README\"\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n10: \n11: Operator and maintenance scripts for this skill.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: ### Purpose\n18: \n19: This README documents the code-bearing folder `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy` so operators can understand its role without opening every source file first. It follows the sk-doc skill README structure while staying focused on code navigation.\n20: \n21: ### Usage\n22: \n23: Use this file to identify the folder boundary, the likely verification path and the local source files that need sk-code conventions. Keep behavior details in source comments and higher-level workflow details in the owning `SKILL.md`.\n24: \n25: ### Key Statistics\n26: \n27: | Metric | Value |\n28: |---|---:|\n29: | Code files | 9 |\n30: | README scope | Direct files in this folder |\n31: | Audit context | Internal validation notes |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 2. QUICK START\n36: \n37: **Step 1: Confirm the owner.**\n38: \n39: Start with `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md` for runtime routing and workflow boundaries.\n40: \n41: **Step 2: Inspect the local code.**\n42: \n43: ```bash\n44: rg --files .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Expected result: the command lists the source files summarized below.\n48: \n49: **Step 3: Verify changes.**\n50: \n51: Run individual scripts from the repository root with the documented arguments.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. FEATURES\n56: \n57: | Feature | What It Does |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Folder boundary | Documents direct code files under `runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy`. |\n60: | sk-code alignment | Points reviewers at OpenCode naming, header, error-handling and type-discipline checks. |\n61: | Verification handoff | Records the expected owner and audit packet for follow-up work. |\n62: \n63: ### Attribution on every run line\n64: \n65: Each line a behaviour run appends carries three fields beside the locator, exit and changed paths:\n66: \n67: - `offeredCommand` is the slash command the advisor head named, or `null` when the head named none.\n68: - `offeredPolicy` is the policy that directly follows that command, one of `run`, `start` or `ask`, or `null` when no policy follows it.\n69: - `briefDelivered` says whether the advisor brief reached the session. It is `true` or `false` where the runtime keeps a readable record, and `unknown` where none exists or cannot be read, so a gap in the record never reads as a miss.\n70: \n71: The offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn instead of a copy of the live session head. The head is the only text that names both the command and the policy, and not every runtime keeps it, so `replayOffer` runs the checkout's own hook and records what that checkout would have offered at the time of the run. A replay that finds no hook, a failing hook or a head without an offer writes null fields plus an `offerError` of `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`, so one bad line never aborts a run.\n72: \n73: ### Backfill a finished log\n74: \n75: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` adds those three fields to a log written before they existed, and a `--backfill` run without `--out` is a usage error that exits 2. The mode refuses with exit 2 and a JSON body holding its `code`:\n76: \n77: - `same_path` when `--out` resolves to the input log, because the backfill writes a new file.\n78: - `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when the corpus refuses a row id, because every prompt is resolved before the first line is written and a refused row leaves no output file behind.\n79: \n80: A line whose locator names no checkout keeps null offer fields and `offerError` `no_checkout`, and the rest of the log is still written with `offerBackfilled: true`.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n85: \n86: | Path | Purpose |\n87: |---|---|\n88: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n89: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n90: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n91: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. |\n92: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. `--row-id <id>` narrows either mode to one row and `--json` prints machine-readable output. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerUnknown`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n93: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input. |\n94: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy` and `briefDelivered` instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), or a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`). |\n95: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n100: \n101: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n102: |---|---|---|\n103: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n104: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n105: \n106: ---\n107: \n108: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n109: \n110: **Audit this folder**\n111: \n112: ```text\n113: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n114: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n115: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n116: ```\n117: \n118: **Score captured runs**\n119: \n120: ```bash\n121: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n122:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n123: ```\n124: \n125: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n126: \n127: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n128: \n129: ```bash\n130: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n131: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n132:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n133: ```\n134: \n135: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n136: \n137: **Backfill a finished log**\n138: \n139: ```bash\n140: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n141:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n142: ```\n143: \n144: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n145: \n146: ---\n147: \n148: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n149: \n150: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n151: |---|---|---|\n152: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n153: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n158: \n159: | Document | Purpose |\n160: |---|---|\n161: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n162: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n163: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794540803403,"end":1794540803842}}}}
